Promoted from the "To append next" paragraph at the bottom of task 126 (number 125), which was recorded as investigation notes rather than commitments and so never became tracked work.

Why: every run so far used a large window, so no benchmark could say anything about pruning behaviour, compaction frequency, re-read rates, or context-loss recovery. Evidence: test1_1/test1_2/test1_3 never compacted at all (peak context 187,624 / 155,274 / 175,572 prompt tokens against a 1,048,576 window), and only test1_qwen exercised compaction, once, at 210,046 reported tokens against a 262,144 window (context.compacted: 44 units, 119,169 tokens shadowed, trigger pressure, strategy summary).

Deliverable: a small-context variant of the same Tetris prompt run under a model or binding whose declared window is about 32k, recorded the same way (project aworkit/test1_small, plus a qa/cache-usage-report.mjs read and the same independent conformance harness).

What it must be able to answer:
- how often compaction fires and what one compaction costs in cold input tokens (the compaction auxiliary call is currently cold: test1_qwen reported sentBytes 540,125 with commonPrefixBytes 3 — see the compaction task for the fix);
- re-read rate: how often the model re-reads a file it already read because context was dropped, and whether the tool loop's read paging keeps those reads small;
- whether a compaction ever loses work that must be redone, measured by the number of turns after each compaction and by whether the deliverable still passes the conformance harness;
- whether the pressure threshold (compaction fired at ~80% of the reported window) is safe under a small window where the summarisation call's own 8k-token output is a much larger share.

Note: usable only with a provider whose cache behaviour is also recorded, otherwise the cache cost of a compaction cannot be separated from the rest.
